+/// BSD `sed` stand-in (gh-1649): BSD sed's `-i` consumes the NEXT argument
+/// as the backup suffix, so GNU's suffix-less `sed -i "s/…" file` cannot
+/// run there — the program text becomes the suffix and the file becomes
